Act as a senior QA engineer, software architect, UX/UI specialist, security analyst, accessibility auditor, performance engineer, and product manager.

Your task is to perform a complete end-to-end audit of my web application, including the desktop, tablet, mobile, frontend, backend/API, database integration, UI/UX, security, accessibility, performance, business logic, and every possible user flow.

Do not limit the audit to obvious bugs. Think like:

* A real user trying to use the application normally.
* A confused or inexperienced user.
* A user intentionally entering incorrect data.
* A malicious user attempting to abuse functionality.
* A QA engineer trying to break every workflow.
* A developer reviewing the code and architecture.
* A product manager looking for opportunities to improve the product.

Do not modify the application. Audit it first and report your findings.

⸻

1. Understand the Application

Before testing, understand:

* The application’s purpose.
* Target users.
* Main features.
* Pages and components.
* Navigation.
* User roles and permissions.
* Frontend architecture.
* Backend/API architecture.
* Database interactions.
* External services/dependencies.
* Authentication/session handling.
* Business rules.
* Data flows.
* Important assumptions.

Map the major user journeys and identify how different features depend on each other.

If something is unclear, infer the intended behavior from the existing implementation and clearly state the assumption.

⸻

2. Complete Functional Testing

Test every feature and workflow, including:

* Happy paths.
* Empty states.
* First-time users.
* Returning users.
* Invalid input.
* Missing input.
* Incorrect formats.
* Very long input.
* Very short input.
* Zero values.
* Negative values.
* Decimal values.
* Extremely large values.
* Special characters.
* Unicode.
* Emojis.
* Arabic.
* English.
* Mixed Arabic/English.
* Duplicate data.
* Conflicting data.
* Boundary values.
* Rapid repeated clicks.
* Double submissions.
* Refresh during operations.
* Browser back/forward.
* Direct URL access.
* Multiple tabs.
* Multiple windows.
* Interrupted workflows.
* Logout/login.
* Session expiration.
* Network failures.
* API failures.
* Backend failures.
* Database failures.
* Missing/invalid responses.
* Data persistence after refresh.
* Data persistence after reopening the application.

For every feature, ask:

“What happens if the user does the opposite of what the developer expected?”

⸻

3. Test Complete User Journeys

Test the application from beginning to end as a real user.

Look for:

* Dead ends.
* Broken navigation.
* Users getting stuck.
* Missing confirmation.
* Missing error messages.
* Unexpected state changes.
* Data loss.
* Actions that cannot be undone.
* Actions that should require confirmation but don’t.
* Unnecessary steps.
* Inconsistent behavior.
* Confusing terminology.
* Poor onboarding.
* Poor empty states.
* Poor loading states.
* Poor success states.
* Poor failure states.
* Unclear next steps.

Separate technical bugs from UX problems.

⸻

4. Mobile & Responsive Testing

Audit the application across:

* Desktop.
* Laptop.
* Tablet.
* Mobile portrait.
* Mobile landscape.
* Small mobile screens.
* Large mobile screens.

Check:

* Responsive layout.
* Text wrapping.
* Buttons.
* Forms.
* Tables.
* Cards.
* Navigation.
* Sidebars.
* Modals.
* Dropdowns.
* Sticky/fixed elements.
* Horizontal scrolling.
* Overflow.
* Touch targets.
* Mobile keyboard behavior.
* Browser behavior.
* Safe areas/notches.
* Orientation changes.
* Elements being clipped.
* Elements overlapping.
* Controls becoming unusable.

Do not assume desktop functionality means mobile functionality works.

⸻

5. UI/UX Audit

Review every page and component for:

* Alignment.
* Spacing.
* Typography.
* Font sizes.
* Visual hierarchy.
* Consistency.
* Colors.
* Contrast.
* Buttons.
* Hover states.
* Focus states.
* Disabled states.
* Loading states.
* Error states.
* Success states.
* Empty states.
* Icons.
* Labels.
* Forms.
* Modals.
* Navigation.
* Information density.

Check whether users can immediately understand:

* What is clickable.
* What is selected.
* What is required.
* What happened.
* Whether an action succeeded.
* Whether an action failed.
* What they should do next.

Identify inconsistencies between pages and components.

⸻

6. Accessibility Audit

Check:

* Keyboard navigation.
* Focus visibility.
* Tab order.
* Screen-reader compatibility.
* Semantic HTML.
* Form labels.
* Error messages.
* ARIA usage.
* Color contrast.
* Color-only indicators.
* Touch target sizes.
* Heading hierarchy.
* Alternative text.
* Modal accessibility.
* Keyboard traps.
* Reduced-motion considerations.
* Browser zoom.
* Text scaling.
* RTL accessibility.

Identify anything that could prevent users with disabilities from using the application.

⸻

7. Arabic & RTL Testing

If Arabic is supported, perform a dedicated RTL audit.

Check:

* Text direction.
* Layout direction.
* Icons.
* Navigation.
* Tables.
* Forms.
* Numbers.
* Dates.
* Currency.
* Mixed Arabic/English content.
* Alignment.
* Dropdowns.
* Modals.
* Buttons.
* Charts.
* Long Arabic text.
* Arabic + English mixed strings.

Look specifically for components that work in LTR but break in RTL.

⸻

8. Data & State Management

Test:

* State after refresh.
* State after navigation.
* State after logout/login.
* State after closing/reopening the browser.
* State after failed requests.
* State after successful requests.
* Stale data.
* Duplicate records.
* Race conditions.
* Concurrent actions.
* Multiple tabs.
* Local storage.
* Session storage.
* Cached data.
* Incorrect defaults.
* Frontend/backend synchronization.

Identify situations where the UI displays something different from the actual backend/database state.

⸻

9. Backend/API/Database Audit

Where applicable, inspect:

* API requests.
* API responses.
* Validation.
* Authentication.
* Authorization.
* Database operations.
* Error handling.
* Data integrity.
* Database relationships.
* Constraints.
* Duplicate submissions.
* Race conditions.
* Excessive API data exposure.
* Incorrect HTTP methods.
* Missing server-side validation.
* Client-side-only validation.
* Sensitive data exposure.

If you discover secrets, API keys, credentials, tokens, or other sensitive information, do not reproduce them in the report. Identify the location and recommend secure remediation.

⸻

10. Security Audit

Perform a defensive security review for:

* XSS.
* Injection vulnerabilities.
* Broken authentication.
* Broken authorization.
* IDOR/BOLA.
* Insecure object access.
* Sensitive information exposure.
* Insecure storage.
* Weak session handling.
* CSRF issues where relevant.
* Unsafe URL handling.
* Open redirects.
* File upload risks.
* Dependency vulnerabilities.
* Excessive permissions.
* Exposed environment variables.
* Debug information.
* CORS configuration.
* Security headers.
* HTTPS issues.
* Client-side secrets.

Do not perform destructive exploitation.

For every security issue, explain:

* What the vulnerability is.
* Where it exists.
* Why it matters.
* Potential impact.
* Recommended remediation.

⸻

11. Performance Audit

Look for:

* Slow initial loading.
* Large JavaScript bundles.
* Large images.
* Excessive API requests.
* Repeated API calls.
* Blocking operations.
* Slow rendering.
* Excessive DOM manipulation.
* Memory leaks.
* Inefficient queries where visible.
* Poor caching.
* Unnecessary re-renders.
* Performance degradation with large datasets.

Consider behavior with:

* 10 records.
* 100 records.
* 1,000 records.
* 10,000+ records where applicable.

Identify scalability problems.

⸻

12. Browser Compatibility

Consider:

* Chrome.
* Safari.
* Edge.
* Firefox.
* iOS Safari.
* Android Chrome.

Identify browser-specific HTML, CSS, JavaScript, API, or rendering issues.

⸻

13. Failure & Recovery Testing

Test what happens when:

* API is unavailable.
* Database is unavailable.
* Request times out.
* Server responds slowly.
* Server returns an error.
* Server returns invalid data.
* Authentication fails.
* Authorization fails.
* User goes offline.
* Network reconnects.
* Browser refreshes during submission.
* User closes the page during an operation.

The application should fail gracefully and avoid silent failures or data loss.

⸻

14. Business Logic & Edge Cases

Test:

* Minimum values.
* Maximum values.
* Date boundaries.
* Month/year transitions.
* Leap years where relevant.
* Duplicate entities.
* Conflicting records.
* Deleted records.
* Missing records.
* Partial data.
* Concurrent modifications.
* Calculations.
* Totals.
* Percentages.
* Rounding.
* Currency calculations.
* Sorting.
* Filtering.
* Searching.
* Pagination.

Verify that displayed calculations match the underlying data.

⸻

